argus-tty (1.0.9) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Found via a real production debugging session: a box where only one
    account (ubuntu, via a personal ~/.aws/credentials file) could upload
    session recordings to S3, because argus-tty-finalize runs as the
    connecting developer/admin/deployer — never as root — so a per-account
    credentials file only ever authenticates that one account, not the
    fleet. The correct fix is an EC2 instance role (every account resolves
    it automatically via the metadata service), but that's not always
    available (e.g. the EC2 instance and S3 bucket are in different AWS
    accounts with no cross-account role + bucket policy set up).
  * reconfigure/status's S3 checks now distinguish "no credentials resolve
    at all" (almost always: no instance role attached) from "credentials
    resolve but access still fails" (role attached, policy needs fixing)
    — these need different fixes, and were previously one generic warning.
  * New AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED/AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_PATH in
    config.env: an explicit, documented fallback for when an instance role
    genuinely isn't an option. reconfigure manages one credentials file
    (root:webapps, 640 — every onboarded account is already a webapps
    member, no new group needed) and wires every account to it via
    /etc/environment, idempotently. Never generates or overwrites real
    credential content, only creates an empty placeholder once.
  * README: new "Configuring S3 upload" section covering the instance-role
    setup (including the cross-account role + bucket policy case) and the
    shared-credentials fallback, with its trade-off stated plainly (one
    static key shared across every account — SSH session attribution is
    unaffected, only the AWS-side upload identity becomes shared).

 -- Ram Laxman Yadav <yadavramlaxman@gmail.com>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 19:04:19 +0530

argus-tty (1.0.5) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Fix: ensure_required_packages (install.sh/reconfigure.sh auto-install)
    used `dpkg -s` to check whether a package was present, but `dpkg -s`
    exits 0 even for a package that's been removed with its conffiles left
    behind ("deinstall ok config-files") — only dpkg having NO record of
    the package at all makes it fail. That meant a genuinely-missing
    required package (e.g. curl removed by hand after install) could be
    silently treated as present and never reinstalled. Now checks the
    actual dpkg-query Status field for "install ok installed". Found via a
    real removal/reconfigure test in a Ubuntu 26.04 container.
  * Fix: `argus-tty list-ip` silently printed only the header row and
    exited 1 with no error, because of `set -euo pipefail` plus `grep`
    exiting 1 for its very common no-match case (an account with no IP
    restriction) inside a `$(...)` assignment — the whole script aborted
    the instant it reached the first unrestricted account, which is most
    fleets. Same latent bug existed in reconfigure.sh's log_output/logfile
    sudoers-line stripping (an edge case, not hit in normal use). Both now
    guarded with `|| true`.
  * Bump Standards-Version to 4.7.3 (lintian: out-of-date-standards-version).
    debhelper-compat stays at 12, and the Priority/Rules-Requires-Root
    fields stay explicit, despite lintian's pedantic suggestions otherwise
    — both are deliberate for staying buildable on older series (focal/
    jammy) this PPA still serves; see debian/changelog's 1.0.0 entry.
  * add-developer.sh now calls retrofit_webapps_rvm_membership directly on
    every onboard (new helper, factored out of reconfigure.sh's inline
    retrofit loop into lib/common.sh), so an existing account re-run
    through `argus-tty add` picks up webapps/rvm membership immediately —
    previously only a separate `argus-tty reconfigure` did this.
    reconfigure.sh's own retrofit step now just calls the same function.
  * Verified end-to-end in a real Ubuntu 26.04 "resolute" container:
    shellcheck clean (warning+) across every shipped script, `lintian`
    clean at default severity, dpkg-buildpackage source build, apt install
    of the built .deb, install.sh's manual path on a box missing
    openssh-server/curl/bsdutils/acl/logrotate (confirms auto-install
    actually recovers them), a real SSH login through the wrapper
    (interactive and non-interactive) with session recording confirmed in
    .tty/.meta, and a full export → remove accounts → import round-trip
    across all three tiers (developer/admin/deployer), including a
    multi-key account and an allow-ip restriction.

 -- Ram Laxman Yadav <yadavramlaxman@gmail.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:14:49 +0530

argus-tty (1.0.4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * install.sh and reconfigure.sh now auto-install every package argus-tty
    depends on (openssh-server, sudo, passwd, bsdutils, e2fsprogs, curl,
    logrotate, systemd, acl) via apt-get if missing, instead of just
    warning — a fresh host no longer needs manual pre-install before
    either path. acl is new here (needed for setfacl, used for the
    per-file ACL grant on a deployed app's shared/.env) and has been
    added to Depends: in debian/control and packaging/DEBIAN/control too.
  * All apt-get calls now go through a shared helper
    (DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive, -qq -o Dpkg::Use-Pty=0, stdin from
    /dev/null) so installs never block on a TTY prompt and don't flood
    captured logs (cloud-init, CI) with progress-bar control characters.
    apt-get update now runs first (non-fatal) so a stale package index no
    longer fails the install outright.
  * Add `argus-tty export`/`import`: dump every developer/admin/deployer
    account (all tiers, every authorized_keys entry, any allow-ip
    restriction) to a single portable roster file and replay it on
    another machine — for cloning a host's actual accounts instead of
    hand-rolling a roster file. Never carries private keys or Google
    Authenticator (TOTP) secrets.

